<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/create_implementation.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-create-implementation">
 <indexterm zone="sql-create-implementation">
  <primary>CREATE IMPLEMENTATION</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE IMPLEMENTATION</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE IMPLEMENTATION</refname>
  <refpurpose>register an alternative handler for an existing index access method</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE IMPLEMENTATION <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable>
    FOR ACCESS METHOD <replaceable class="parameter">am_name</replaceable>
    HANDLER <replaceable class="parameter">handler_function</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE IMPLEMENTATION</command> registers a
   handler for an existing index access method.  It is intended for
   extensions that want to supply their own handler extending or replacing
   existing one from existing access method without changing the access
   method's name and functionality for all other callers.
  </para>

  <para>
   An index created without an <literal>IMPLEMENTATION</literal> clause
   continues to use the handler from <link
   linkend="catalog-pg-am"><structname>pg_am</structname></link>.  A
   newly created index that names an implementation uses the handler
   recorded in <link
   linkend="catalog-pg-amimpl"><structname>pg_amimpl</structname></link>
   instead.
  </para>

  <para>
   Implementations apply only to <emphasis>index</emphasis> access
   methods.  The handler function must return type
   <type>index_am_handler</type>.
  </para>

  <para>
   You must be a superuser to create an implementation.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of the implementation to be created.  Implementation
      names are unique across the database.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">am_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of an existing index access method.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">handler_function</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name (optionally schema-qualified) of a previously registered
      function that implements the index access method.  The function
      must take a single argument of type <type>internal</type> and
      return type <type>index_am_handler</type>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Register an alternative btree handler supplied by an extension:
<programlisting>
CREATE IMPLEMENTATION btree_oriole FOR ACCESS METHOD btree
    HANDLER orioledb_btree_handler;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Use the implementation when creating an index:
<programlisting>
CREATE INDEX ON t USING btree (col) IMPLEMENTATION btree_oriole;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE IMPLEMENTATION</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>See Also</title>

  <simplelist type="inline">
   <member><xref linkend="sql-alter-implementation"/></member>
   <member><xref linkend="sql-drop-implementation"/></member>
